This paper seeks to derive optimal estimates for the first Robin eigenvalue of the anisotropic $p$-Laplace operator, defined as:
\begin{equation*}
\lambda_1(\beta,\Omega)=\min_{\psi\in W^{1,p}(\Omega)\setminus\{0\} }
\frac{\displaystyle\int_\Omega F(\nabla \psi)^p dx +\beta
\displaystyle\int_{\partial\Omega}|\psi|^pF(\nu_{\Omega}) d\mathcal H^{N-1}
}{\displaystyle\int_\Omega|\psi|^p dx}, \end{equation*}
where $p\in]1,+\infty[$, $\Omega$ is a bounded, mean convex domain in $\mathbb{R}^{N}$, $\nu_{\Omega}$ denotes its Euclidean outward normal, $\beta$ is a real number, and $F$ represents a sufficiently smooth norm on $\mathbb{R}^{N}$. Our derived estimates are expressed through the first eigenvalue of a related one-dimensional nonlinear problem, which varies with $\beta$ and specific geometric properties of $\Omega$. Specifically, we establish a lower bound for $\lambda_{1}$ when $\beta>0$ and an upper bound when $\beta<0$. Consequently, for $\beta>0$, we prove a lower bound for $\lambda_{1}(\beta,\Omega)$ based on the anisotropic inradius of $\Omega$, while for $\beta<0$, we demonstrate an upper bound for $\lambda_{1}(\beta,\Omega)$ as a function of $\beta$.

This paper investigates a Nirenberg-type problem on the standard half-sphere $(\mathbb{S}^n_+,g_0)$, focusing on the existence of conformal metrics with prescribed scalar curvature and vanishing boundary mean curvature. This objective translates into solving a specific boundary value problem featuring the critical Sobolev exponent:
\begin{equation*}
(\mathcal{P}) \quad \begin{cases}
  -\D_{g_0} u \, + \, \frac{n(n-2)}{4} u \, = K \, u^{\frac{n+2}{n-2}},\, u > 0
& \mbox{in } \mathbb{S}^n_+,
  \frac{\partial u}{\partial \nu }\, =\, 0 & \mbox{on } \partial
\mathbb{S}^n_+. \end{cases} \end{equation*}
where $K$ is a positive function in $C^2(\mathbb{S}^n_+)$. Under generic conditions on $K$, we establish finite energy solutions for a subcritical approximation of $(\mathcal{P})$ on half-spheres with dimension $n \geq 5$. These solutions display multiple blow-ups of a \emph{cluster-type} occurring at a single boundary point. Such solutions can possess zero or non-zero weak limits and may form clusters at various boundary locations. This blow-up behavior on half-spheres stands in sharp contrast to the classical Nirenberg problem on spheres, where non-simple blow-ups for finite energy subsolutions are impossible. Furthermore, this phenomenon reveals an unexpected link to vortex-type problems in Euler equations from fluid dynamics and mean-field equations in mathematical physics. Additionally, under appropriate conditions on the restriction of $K$ to the boundary $\partial \mathbb{S}^n_+$, we construct approximate solutions with arbitrarily large energy and Morse index.

This study addresses the challenge of sampling a $k$-clique from a graph according to an almost uniform distribution within sublinear time, operating under the general graph query model. The goal is for the algorithm to output any specific $k$-clique with probability $(1\pm \epsilon)/n_k$, where $n_k$ represents the total count of $k$-cliques in the graph and $\epsilon$ serves as the approximation parameter. We establish that the query complexity for this task is \[
\Theta^*\left(\max\left\{ \left(\frac{(n\alpha)^{k/2}}{
n_k}\right)^{\frac{1}{k-1}} ,\; \min\left\{n\alpha,\frac{n\alpha^{k-1}}{n_k}
\right\}\right\}\right), \] with $n$ denoting the number of vertices, $\alpha$ the arboricity, and $\Theta^*$ indicating that the dependence on $(\log n/\epsilon)^{O(k)}$ is suppressed. Notably, this result highlights a divergence between approximate counting and approximate uniform sampling in the sublinear setting. For instance, when $k=3$, $\alpha = O(1)$, and the number of triangles $n_3$ is $\Theta(n)$, our lower bound yields $\Omega(n^{1/4})$ (for constant $\epsilon$), whereas a $(1\pm \epsilon)$-approximation of $n_3$ can be achieved using only $\textrm{poly}(\log(n/\epsilon))$ queries (Eden, Ron, and Seshadhri, SODA20). Our lower bound stems from constructing a family of graphs with arboricity $\alpha$ containing $n_k$ cliques of size $k$, where one clique is "hidden" and thus difficult to sample. Conversely, our upper bound relies on defining a specialized auxiliary graph $H_k$, such that nearly uniform edge sampling in $H_k$ corresponds to nearly uniform $k$-clique sampling in the original graph $G$. We then leverage a known edge-sampling algorithm (Eden, Ron, and Rosenbaum, ICALP19) to sample edges in $H_k$, addressing the difficulty of simulating queries to $H_k$ given only access to $G$.

This paper derives enhanced Hölder regularity properties for weak solutions to nonlocal problems governed by the fractional double phase operator:
\begin{align*}
\mc L u(x):=&2 \; {\rm P.V.} \int_{\mathbb R^N}
\frac{|u(x)-u(y)|^{p-2}(u(x)-u(y))}{|x-y|^{N+ps_1}}dy \nonumber
  &+2 \; {\rm P.V.} \int_{\mathbb R^N} a(x,y)
\frac{|u(x)-u(y)|^{q-2}(u(x)-u(y))}{|x-y|^{N+qs_2}}dy,
  \end{align*}
where $1<p\leq q<\infty$, $0<s_2, s_1<1$, and the modulating coefficient $a(\cdot,\cdot)$ is a non-negative bounded function. Specifically, we demonstrate improved space-time Hölder continuity for weak solutions to time-dependent nonlocal double phase problems within a specific subclass of modulating coefficients. Furthermore, by employing appropriate approximation techniques, we establish higher (global) Hölder continuity results for weak solutions to the stationary versions of these problems involving the operator $\mc L$ when the modulating coefficients are locally continuous.

This study examines a specific class of fractional Sobolev critical nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) coupled systems defined by:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\begin{array}{lll} (-\Delta)^{s} u=\mu_{1}
u+|u|^{2^{*}_{s}-2}u+\eta_{1}|u|^{p-2}u+\gamma\alpha|u|^{\alpha-2}u|v|^{\beta}
~ \text{in}~ \mathbb{R}^{N},\\ (-\Delta)^{s} v=\mu_{2}
v+|v|^{2^{*}_{s}-2}v+\eta_{2}|v|^{q-2}v+\gamma\beta|u|^{\alpha}|v|^{\beta-2}v
~~\text{in}~ \mathbb{R}^{N},\\ \|u\|^{2}_{L^{2}}=m_{1}^{2} ~\text{and}~
\|v\|^{2}_{L^{2}}=m_{2}^{2}, \end{array}\right. \end{equation*}
where $(-\Delta)^{s}$ denotes the fractional Laplacian, with parameters $N>2s$ and $s\in(0,1)$. The constants $\mu_{1}$ and $\mu_{2}$ are unknown Lagrange multipliers, while $\eta_{1}, \eta_{2}, \gamma, m_{1}, m_{2}$ are positive, and $\alpha, \beta > 1$. The exponents satisfy $p, q, \alpha+\beta\in(2+4s/N,2^{*}_{s}]$, where $2^{*}_{s}$ represents the fractional Sobolev critical index. Our primary findings are as follows: first, when $p, q, \alpha+\beta$ are strictly less than $2^{*}_{s}$, we establish the existence of positive normalized solutions provided $\gamma$ is sufficiently large. Second, in the case where $p=q=\alpha+\beta=2^{*}_{s}$, we demonstrate that no positive normalized solutions exist. The core methodologies employed in this work include scaling transformations, case-by-case classification discussions, and the concentration-compactness principle.

